    "q-304018": {
      "dom_id": "step-q304018",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=4{e}^{x}+17$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $17$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{x}+17) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(17) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} & ={(4{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
